// 004.12, 012.1 (D40, #73): when two otherwise-unranked `Default`/`Exception` rules are
// grounded in `Document`s with distinct `Rank`s (`Rank 1` > `Rank 2`, smaller integer = higher
// authority), `Resolve` synthesizes the `lex superior` priority edge so the higher-ranked
// document's rule prevails without a normative conflict, while explicit `Overrides`/`Priority`
// edges and same-rank conflicts (`T21`) remain intact.
Document TENANCY "include/tenancy.md" Rank 1.
Document BYLAW "include/bylaw.md" Rank 2.

Bool active.

Bool obligation-applies
  Default statute-rule Holds when active
  Default bylaw-rule Rejects when active.

Bool same-rank-check
  Default r-yes Holds when active
  Default r-no Rejects when active.

Clause T.1 In TENANCY Approved "86b3e3ec5210d42d759e02e1685609c84eab9d45d45774634f7cbfd6e7ffa149"
  Norm "The tenant pays the rent on the first of each month." => statute-rule, r-yes, r-no.

Clause B.1 In BYLAW Approved "6ac4054cc9ee87ed421c9de60e053a45d961a994c92f0eeaf41eda8550cdc754"
  Norm "The board may authorize a share issuance only when quorum holds." => bylaw-rule.

Scenario higher-rank-prevails-over-lower-rank {
  +active.
  ?!Conflict(obligation-applies()).
  ?obligation-applies.
}

Scenario explicit-priority-can-delegate-to-lower-rank {
  +active.
  Extend Bool obligation-applies Priority bylaw-rule Over statute-rule.
  ?!Conflict(obligation-applies()).
  ?!obligation-applies.
}

Scenario same-rank-rules-still-conflict {
  +active.
  ?Conflict(same-rank-check()).
  ?!same-rank-check.
}
